; 25-break.kurt

; `break` abandons the current block outright, without trying to prove
; anything -- no impl-intro, no forall-intro, nothing is added to the theory.
; It is the only way to close a `sandbox` block (14-sandbox.kurt), but it
; also works on any other open block, in the shell:
;
;     !!![1] bool A
;     !!![2] show A implies A
;     show A implies A                           ; 2 claim
;     !!![3] proof
;     proof
;     !!!    [4]     break
;     break                                       ; 4 forgot the last block
;     !!![5] A implies A
;     !!!!!! ProofError: can not derive `A implies A`
;
; Like `done` (24-done.kurt), `break` needs `indent` mode (lesson 54) and
; only works in the interactive shell -- a `.kurt` file cannot use it at all.
;
; This file has no runnable `break` for that reason; the fact below just
; keeps it checkable on its own.

bool A
use A
A
